WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:09.050
DAN KOPP: Well, let's talk
about some hurricanes.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.810
So hurricanes,
natural disasters.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.920
They cause a lot of
problems around the world.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.390
One-point-- sorry--
$153.9 billion of damage

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:31.070
in 2016 alone.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:35.452
No one is immune to
weather emergencies.

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:36.285
Are people prepared?

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.700
Whether it's a hurricane,
or it's a tornado,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.880
or it's a flood or a snowstorm,
everybody needs something.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.430
And sometimes people
need a lot of things.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:52.744
But what do you do?

00:00:52.744 --> 00:00:53.910
You go to the grocery store.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.200
The shelves are empty.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.820
You go to your local
hardware store,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.470
and you need a
tarp for your roof,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.595
and they could all be gone.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.780
Empty shelves don't help anyone.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.820
There's a lot of
opportunity around this.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.520
How do organizations
and businesses

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.460
change the way they
operate so they can quickly

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.260
react to these types of storms
to meet people's basic needs?

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.630
Obviously, there's chances
to increase revenue.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:26.350
There's obviously chances to
lower transportation costs.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.980
A lot of times in
these scenarios,

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.064
supply chains get
disrupted, and you end up

00:01:32.064 --> 00:01:33.730
paying what's essentially
the same thing

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.104
as overnight shipping to get
your product to your stores,

00:01:36.104 --> 00:01:38.796
and it costs a lot of money.

00:01:38.796 --> 00:01:41.170
And then you also have a chance
to make a real difference

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.836
in people's lives,
and you have a chance

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:45.310
to really have a lot of
increased customer loyalty.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.652
You can really win
customers for life

00:01:47.652 --> 00:01:49.360
if your organizations
can get this right.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:53.770
But obviously, it's not easy.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.450
There's a lot of real
world constraints

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.030
that come into play here.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.630
You don't have every
product that you

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.110
need in every store and
every distribution center.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:03.580
Like I mentioned, logistics--

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.500
the costs are expensive,
and there's often

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.690
a very short window of time.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:12.670
If you're talking about
a tornado or a wildfire,

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.714
you'd have hours,
maybe, of notice.

00:02:15.714 --> 00:02:17.380
In the case of a
hurricane, maybe you're

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.340
lucky enough that you've
got a couple days.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.680
And so an organization has to
be able to react very quickly.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.790
But you also don't
know which products

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.492
you need to send, and
then how much, and when.

00:02:27.492 --> 00:02:29.450
So that's what we're
going to talk about today.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.070
So quickly, who are we?

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:37.300
We are North Highland.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.500
We are a full spectrum
expertise management consulting

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.670
firm, mid-sized, located
in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.850
And we go to market across
four different service lines--

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:53.020
customer experience, performance
improvement, tech and digital,

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:57.040
and transformation with
these eight capabilities.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.290
And the three of
us presenting today

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.197
are in the data and
analytics capability.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.615
OK, how do you respond to
this as an organization?

00:03:11.412 --> 00:03:14.450
That didn't come out right.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.340
So we're talking
about a model today.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.650
We're talking about
a predictive model.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.870
And with any
predictive model, even

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.900
if it is the most accurate
model in the world,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.440
somebody has to do
something with that number.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.970
Somebody has to operationalize
it and make it happen.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:34.340
And for a large
enterprise, there

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.600
are many components that
have to be pulled together

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.070
to make that happen.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.770
Product prediction, that's what
we're here to talk about today.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:44.370
But other things, such as
ownership and decision-making.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.950
Who is going to say if that's
the right amount of product

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.510
to send to a store?

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.480
What approvals have to happen?

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.230
Having some kind of
central command, where

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.521
people are all in the same
room to make these decisions

00:03:54.521 --> 00:03:56.010
very quickly.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.500
All the different trends
and freight issues

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.830
around diverting freight
from different locations.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:08.460
Having contracts in place for
shipping in these situations,

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.070
so you're not paying
spot market prices.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.770
Obviously, communication and
visibility is very important.

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:17.999
IT system uptime--
everything runs on a system.

00:04:17.999 --> 00:04:19.540
If you can't get
these orders plugged

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.956
into some kind of
system, it's not

00:04:20.956 --> 00:04:23.550
going to result in a truck roll.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.570
And store ops resilience--
this is a big one.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:30.320
So if you've got a store out
there, a hurricane just went by

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.950
and the roof is ripped
off, how do you know?

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:36.710
Are your people going
to be able to show up?

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:40.910
If they can show up and
your store is intact,

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:42.770
customers are going
to start showing up.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.620
What happens if you only have
some of your employees show up,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.450
or they're home
helping their families?

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.370
What if they don't have power?

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:54.800
All these things happen, and so
people need to prepare for it.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.410
And finally,
customer engagement.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.020
If you can do hyper
local communication

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.200
with your customers to say which
stores are open, which stores

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.360
are closed, what
products do they have,

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.370
what products are
they waiting on,

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:11.980
you will definitely resonate
with your customers.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.130
OK, the business case.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.830
There's a lot of
opportunity here.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.090
We looked at a number
of investor calls

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.060
throughout the last
number of years,

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.810
where there have been
a lot of hurricanes.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.601
And you can see there
are big numbers we're

00:05:26.601 --> 00:05:30.120
talking about-- $360 million
of hurricane-related sales

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.710
in 2011.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.630
I believe that was
Hurricane Sandy.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:38.780
Second quote, significant demand
on the transportation system,

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:40.070
and costs went up.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:45.030
This is the C-suite of
very large companies.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.160
Hurricanes Harvey and
Irma drove demand,

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.910
and our team sent over
11,000 truckloads of product

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.640
for two storms in two
cities, two states.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.810
To take that one
step further, we'll

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.010
talk about the actual
revenue impact.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.510
So this is from the
end of last year,

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:10.610
again, for Irma and
Harvey, different retailers

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.830
and the impact it
had on their revenue.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.870
You can see you've got a lot
of home improvement companies

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:16.520
up at the top.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.444
Clearly, they have a
lot of revenue to gain.

00:06:19.444 --> 00:06:21.860
And then you've got some others
lower on the bottom there,

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.530
where maybe they don't--
such as Macy's and Kohl's--

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.770
don't necessarily sell a
lot of generators or tarps

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:30.510
or flashlights and batteries.

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.870
But they are impacted
just the same.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:35.600
Maybe they need to work on
their store ops resilience

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:38.407
or come up with their
transportation plans,

00:06:38.407 --> 00:06:40.240
or they need to adjust
those to reduce cost.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:47.119
But that is not what we really
came to talk about today.

00:06:47.119 --> 00:06:49.160
We want to talk about a
little bit of technology.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.040
And here to go
into the prediction

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:52.451
is my colleague, Charlie Morn.

00:06:52.451 --> 00:06:54.200
CHARLIE MORN: How did
we make this happen?

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:59.860
How did we build a model that
can effectively predict demand

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:05.320
in near real-time, based on
very confusing situations

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:07.660
on the ground?

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.660
Well, Google Cloud
Platform has enough tools

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.780
to make a system like
this possible to build.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.180
Everything from a
big data platform,

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:20.740
data warehousing system in
BigQuery and cloud storage,

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.510
to the machine
learning tools that

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:26.920
are enabled through Cloud
ML, TensorFlow, scikit-learn

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:31.120
and Keras on top of that,
to orchestration layers

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.210
like Kubernetes that lets
you run massive processing

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:37.450
loads at a moment's notice
that can scale up and down

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.660
and be efficient in
their processing.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:45.250
It gives us a very
easily deployable,

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:51.010
maintainable, highly reliable
system that we can work with.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.239
One of the biggest problems
that we had coming in,

00:07:53.239 --> 00:07:55.030
and we knew we were
going to run into this,

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:56.950
was getting all
the weather data.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:01.000
If you don't know where a
storm hit almost exactly,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.250
how can you predict
hyper local demand?

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:05.650
If a hurricane
moves by 50 miles,

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:08.564
you might be sending
generators to the wrong store.

00:08:08.564 --> 00:08:10.480
So you need to understand
exactly where demand

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.260
is going to be by understanding
what the weather is.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.480
There's a big problem
with that, though.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:20.230
Turns out when a
hurricane hits, all

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:23.080
of the meters and sensors
that the National Weather

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:25.360
Service has out there fail.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.520
They stop reporting
accurate wind speeds.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.620
They stop reporting
total rainfall,

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.209
and you just get nulls
in your data set.

00:08:32.209 --> 00:08:34.750
Actually, NOAA has a great data
set that's available for free

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:36.039
in Google BigQuery.

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:37.570
We looked into it,
and it turns out

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.370
during all of the key
hurricanes that we

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.770
were interested in
training on, there

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:44.200
were big old blanks in the data.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:47.560
But no, it does
provide time forecasts,

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.110
and you can get
current hour forecasts

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:52.510
that essentially reflect
the reality on the ground.

00:08:54.937 --> 00:08:56.770
But those are in an
interesting file format,

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.080
and they can't just be dumped
straight into a database.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:00.640
So we need a
processing tool that we

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:07.420
can use to get the data
from NOAA into BigQuery.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:09.430
So how do we do this?

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.710
Each file is
programmatically named

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:15.290
based on the time, the
forecast that it's running,

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:19.210
as well as the number of hours
in the future that the forecast

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:22.340
is for, because if something
is happening right now--

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.600
sorry about that-- if an
event is happening right now,

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.620
we want to be able to understand
the impact in the future

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:30.860
as well.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.630
So we can generate a
URL from the date/time

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:37.420
that we're interested
in predicting,

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:39.730
and download from
NOAA and process

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:44.740
that in-- actually, it's
a Fortran library that has

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:46.870
existed since, I think, 1982.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.425
So we need a
specialized environment

00:09:48.425 --> 00:09:49.300
that can run Fortran.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:52.510
We can't just run this
in a Node App Engine

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:54.970
or something like that.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:58.690
Then we will dump the
results into a CSV,

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:02.020
and stream those
records into BigQuery.

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:06.250
24 files per day for 10 years
results in about 150 billion

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:09.850
records that we're
having to load in order

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:12.070
to train the model.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.010
So we used Docker and
Kubernetes in this case

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:16.752
to do our deployment strategy.

00:10:16.752 --> 00:10:18.460
There are a lot of
different technologies

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:21.430
you could use to do this.

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:25.840
As an organization is moving
into a cloud environment,

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:28.330
they're going to have
guardrails put up.

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:30.310
You have to work within
the constraints that

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:32.800
are set by security and
reality on the ground.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.680
And Kubernetes was
a really good tool

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.530
that was in our
allowable toolbox here.

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:43.190
So we developed
Python programs--

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.050
two of them, actually--

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:50.450
to just do the quick streaming,
and land in Kubernetes.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:55.070
Now if you've been to many
talks this conference,

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:57.110
you've probably heard
about Kubernetes.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:59.690
And most of the talks
are about Kubernetes

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:02.899
as a microservices
handling layer.

00:11:02.899 --> 00:11:04.940
You can deploy a bunch of
different microservices

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.350
on Kubernetes, and they
will scale up, scale down.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:10.100
That's actually not
what we're doing here.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.130
We only have two services
in this, and neither of them

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:13.820
are micro.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:17.550
We're just using Kubernetes
for raw computing power,

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.850
and we don't have
to manage anything.

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:26.450
So we have a Redis
Pod that we spun up.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:31.520
That has a list with all 10,000
files that we want to download.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:36.510
And we spin up about
500 different computers.

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.270
Each processing
task is very small,

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:42.110
so we only need the G1
small compute engine type.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:45.050
It's very inexpensive,
and we can actually

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.690
make those nodes
preemptible as well

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:51.050
to lower the cost even further.

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:55.660
Redis gives us a very fault
tolerant system to work in.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:57.510
It's very fast.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:00.500
And this is all the code that
it took to spin up two jobs.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:04.010
One, on the left, you've
got our Python script

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.482
that's going to do the
downloading and processing.

00:12:06.482 --> 00:12:07.940
And on the right
is just an example

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:10.130
of how you would spin up
the Kubernetes cluster.

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.230
That's pretty much
all the work that we

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:15.440
had to do from the management
and orchestration side.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.510
It saves a lot of time.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.950
It's really clean and
nice to work with,

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.890
and you don't have to know
MapReduce, which is also nice.

00:12:25.849 --> 00:12:27.640
So Matt's going to come
up in just a second

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.370
to talk about the details
of how we do the prediction,

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:34.170
but just to give you an idea
of what we're predicting,

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:39.340
we're looking across 300 product
categories for a retailer.

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:42.610
And we're trying to understand,
what is the change in dollar

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.530
demand in that category?

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:51.340
If I want to buy a mop, how
much is the total dollar demand

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:52.090
going to increase?

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:54.130
Well, you might have
multi packs of mops.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:57.700
You might have single packs of
mops, multi packs of bleach.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:01.216
So by distilling it
to the dollar level,

00:13:01.216 --> 00:13:02.590
you're able to
get a good insight

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:06.460
into what is the actual
consumer need in this area.

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:08.050
How much product
do we need to ship?

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:16.370
And this is a noisy graph,
but I'll explain how it works.

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:20.350
If we look at the way demand
actually works in a store,

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:23.559
the blue line is the
real customer demand.

00:13:23.559 --> 00:13:25.600
These are people coming
and knocking on your door

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:29.560
and saying, I want to
buy something from you.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:32.200
A traditional
replenishment system

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:35.650
is not fast enough to
respond to that demand,

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:37.510
because leading
into an event, you

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:39.880
will have what's
called pre-strike,

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:43.330
where the demand is
increased immediately

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:44.904
before the hurricane comes.

00:13:44.904 --> 00:13:46.570
I bet a lot of people
here have gone out

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:49.330
and bought flashlights
or batteries right

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:52.840
before a hurricane is coming
just in case you lose power.

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:55.390
Additionally, you're going
to have a second demand spike

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.120
when the event really happens.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:00.965
And that's the one that's really
hard to respond to and predict.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:06.640
The yellow line
that you see here

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:09.340
is the amount of
days of stock that

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:13.300
a traditional replenishment
system might leave you with.

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:15.160
Ordinarily, it works well.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:16.690
It keeps you around.

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:20.080
Whatever baseline you set
for your punishment system,

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:23.140
it keeps about that many
days of stock on hand.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:27.470
Maybe you get a shipment
every two weeks of mops.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:30.230
But in these severe
events, that demand

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.630
is going to increase
way too quickly,

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:35.000
and you'll very, very
quickly be out of stock.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:40.790
You won't have what you need on
the shelves for people to buy,

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:44.260
and then you'll have too much.

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.010
After the event, your
replenishment system

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:48.110
has caught up, and
now all of a sudden,

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.750
you've got two or three
times the inventory

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:52.640
that you want to have on hand.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.560
For mops, this may or
may not be a big deal,

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:57.620
but think about a high
cost item like a generator.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:01.460
If you ship a pallet of
generators to the wrong store,

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:05.660
you have inventory that you
can't sell in the wrong place

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:08.390
that you may have to ship to
another store that actually

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:09.140
needs it.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:10.984
But by then it
might be too late.

00:15:10.984 --> 00:15:12.650
People aren't buying
generators anymore,

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:15.290
because their power
is turned back on.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:17.250
And so now you've got
a bunch of extra stock

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.660
that you either have to mark
down significantly or send back

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:25.510
to the distribution center
or something like that.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.910
So we looked into how
we can predict this,

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.570
and we came up with
a lot of options.

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:33.370
We looked at traditional
time series analysis.

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.620
We looked at linear
regressions, things like that.

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:38.740
None of those can handle
the scale and volume of data

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:39.910
that we were considering.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.060
We really needed something
that could be rapidly scaled

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:49.830
up and accurate across the depth
of data that we're looking at.

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:55.710
To give you an insight into
the modeling techniques

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.410
that we used, we're going
to bring up Matt Yates, who

00:15:58.410 --> 00:15:59.520
is my colleague.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:01.770
MATT YATES: I had a lot of
energy around this project,

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:05.100
just because the opportunity to
really impact people's lives,

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.752
to meet people at
a time of need.

00:16:07.752 --> 00:16:09.960
And it was a lot of fun too,
because we got to really

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.340
be on the bleeding edge.

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:14.460
Loved interacting and working
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:16.920
This was one of my
first interactions.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:20.730
So I think that the
two themes that we've

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:23.700
heard about Kubernetes and
about machine learning and AI

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:27.390
at this conference
really continue on,

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:31.080
and we really experienced
the benefits of those things

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:33.070
as we'll go through here.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:35.910
So just to give you a sense
of what our pipeline looks

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.820
like here, we had to build
out a ton of features

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:43.320
in order to be able to give
you an accurate prediction.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:46.250
So we've got features
in here, and we'll

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:49.170
go into really detail around
what we're doing there.

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:52.560
But we've got Kubernetes
pulling in that weather data.

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:54.060
We've got BigQuery,
which is housing

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:56.400
a lot of internal company data.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:59.910
We're augmenting
that with census data

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:05.010
and some other really
rich open data sources,

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:07.710
putting those into
models, training them.

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:09.679
TensorFlow and Keras
have a little bit

00:17:09.679 --> 00:17:11.220
of scikit-learn in
the pipeline there

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:14.010
as well, the pre-processing.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.740
And then ultimately all
of that gets wrapped up

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:20.140
into an output prediction

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:23.290
So let's deep dive
on some of these.

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:27.850
On the actual features,
weather we knew right away.

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:29.350
We're going to have
to collect that.

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:32.860
We went and got billions of
records, put them in BigQuery,

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:39.850
was able to roll those up into
a store hourly grain in BigQuery

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:40.982
in a matter of minutes.

00:17:40.982 --> 00:17:42.940
So you're talking about
terabytes and terabytes

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:45.940
of data, billions of records.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:49.510
We sort of arranged it into
store using trade areas,

00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:50.600
zip codes.

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:54.130
So we needed to go from an
individual weather dot on a map

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:56.410
to a zip code,
assigning it to a store,

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:59.830
and then rolling all of that
up to a store hourly, actual,

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:01.180
or forecasted weather.

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.710
And BigQuery handled
it effortlessly.

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:07.450
It was spectacular.

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:11.190
Beyond that we went
out and got NLCD data.

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.430
That's National
Land Cover Database.

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:17.110
It's a governmental
agency that publishes--

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:19.590
basically, they go
through satellite imagery,

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:23.830
turn that using some
classification tree

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:28.120
models into information around
what's the tree cover like.

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:30.280
What's the impervious
surface percentage

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:34.360
at any particular
point at a three meter

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:37.060
grain across the US?

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:39.070
They also have a really rich--

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:42.040
I think it was 18
category data set about,

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:44.890
for this point on a map,
is this high urban density,

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:46.120
low urban density?

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:49.720
Is it ice, or is it a farmland?

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:52.630
So some really rich
data around what's

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:54.605
happening on the earth
at a particular point.

00:18:54.605 --> 00:18:56.980
And we were able to aggregate
that off at the store level

00:18:56.980 --> 00:19:00.220
to say, what percentage
of the store's trade area

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:03.675
is covered by farmland versus
high density urban housing?

00:19:03.675 --> 00:19:05.800
And that's going to make
a difference in what sells

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:09.370
ultimately following a storm.

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:12.370
Census data-- widely available.

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:17.260
Population densities pulled in
a lot of really cool information

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.630
from the census as
a potential feature.

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:22.767
And then inventory sales
data from our client.

00:19:27.005 --> 00:19:29.130
So one thing that I really
wanted to point out here

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:31.870
was we did almost all of
our feature engineering

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:36.600
in BigQuery, and I absolutely
loved working with it.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.511
SQL worked 95% of the time.

00:19:39.511 --> 00:19:41.010
We found a couple
of instances where

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:44.380
we wanted to use JavaScript
in a user-defined function.

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:46.470
So I've got a
little example here.

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:48.450
Our project was
beyond hurricanes,

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.940
so this is a tornado example.

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:54.480
But it would be pretty
difficult in SQL alone

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:59.010
to, given a latitude-longitude
of a tornado,

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:00.720
find all of the
stores associated

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:04.100
with potential impact from that
tornado, find their distance,

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:05.560
rank the distance.

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:08.095
It takes some pretty complex
geometry, trigonometry.

00:20:08.095 --> 00:20:10.470
I had to go back and remember
some stuff from high school

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:14.610
to do this, but I was able to
create a user-defined function,

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.860
call that user-defined
function in SQL,

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:20.250
and create some really
complex calculations

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:22.770
right on top of the query.

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:23.590
Very fun stuff.

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:31.390
So then once we did our
feature engineering,

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:33.010
we needed to train those models.

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:35.050
We went in here thinking
we could sort of create

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:37.060
one model to rule them all.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:38.920
So we're thinking RNNs.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:40.330
We have some time series.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:41.584
We're thinking multi-output.

00:20:41.584 --> 00:20:43.250
So we have all these
product categories.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:45.490
Let's predict them
all in a single model.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:47.120
We experimented with that.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:49.990
We were able to get
a skillful model.

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:52.120
It was about twice
as good as a naive

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.500
model of just a moving average.

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:56.050
So if a moving
average model said

00:20:56.050 --> 00:21:03.440
you'd have $200 worth of cells,
we might be $200 off for that.

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:06.970
And our model with our RNN
was able to predict that

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:08.140
twice as well.

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:09.610
But we weren't
satisfied with that.

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:11.770
We thought, we can do
better if we dissect

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:13.060
the problem a little more.

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:15.660
So ultimately, we
ended up creating

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:19.540
just a sequential
feedforward neural network

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:21.310
and actually dividing
it from multi-output

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:23.430
down to a single
output, and then

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:26.170
just creating a lot of
those little deep networks

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:28.270
and calling them individually.

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:30.460
And when we create
the prediction,

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:33.310
we can call each of those
models individually in serial

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:35.119
or parallel, and
we're able to make

00:21:35.119 --> 00:21:36.910
those predictions for
each product category

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:41.770
using a similarly
architected neural network,

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:45.240
but with the discrete weights.

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:48.010
So initially, we're
using TensorFlow.

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:51.600
We were feeding the data
in with a queue runner,

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.600
because with RNNs, you've
got pretty complex data sets,

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.700
very big data sets.

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:58.470
Ultimately, we
found if we wanted

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:00.600
to try and make this as
skillful as possible,

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:01.990
let's try to iterate fast.

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:05.490
So in comes Keras.

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:07.860
We're able to iterate and
try things out in Keras.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:10.170
It's fully compatible with
ML Engine, which we'll

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:12.880
talk about here in a second.

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:18.330
So we moved from pure
TensorFlow to a Keras library

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:21.110
on top of that with
a TensorFlow back end

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:22.738
to do some rapid prototyping.

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:28.040
And the way that we sort of
reframed the problem here

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:32.000
is instead of trying to do an
RNN and a time series analysis,

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.190
we said, every one
of these events

00:22:34.190 --> 00:22:37.520
has a time zero when the
event actually happened.

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:40.310
We need to go figure out
what our baseline sales were

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:41.750
in the days coming up to that.

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:44.540
We need to figure out what
our pre-strike sales are,

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:45.804
if that's applicable.

00:22:45.804 --> 00:22:47.720
And that's something we
might want to predict.

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:50.500
And we would also predict
our post-strike sales.

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:53.240
And the good news is
with a weather forecast,

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:56.000
if I trust the forecast
of the weather,

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:57.100
and I'm not trying to--

00:22:57.100 --> 00:22:58.850
we weren't trying to
forecast the weather.

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:02.150
We were consuming that from
NOAA, the National Oceanic

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:04.360
Atmospheric Administration.

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:07.504
I can actually, days ahead of
a hurricane actually hitting,

00:23:07.504 --> 00:23:09.920
I can give you, according to
the weather forecast, where's

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:12.128
that hurricane going to hit,
and go ahead and predict

00:23:12.128 --> 00:23:14.270
the post-strike demand
right after that.

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:16.110
And then we pooled
the time together,

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:21.960
so instead of trying to do a
time series demand forecast,

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:23.790
we said, let's take
all the demand that's

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.640
going to happen in the immediate
period following the hurricane,

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:29.549
lump it all together, and
say, after this hurricane,

00:23:29.549 --> 00:23:31.090
here's going to be
your demand level.

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:33.030
And it's going to be
spread out across time,

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:35.876
but we kind of divorced the
time element from our problem.

00:23:35.876 --> 00:23:38.000
We were able to get a more
skillful model that way.

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:45.600
So then once we got our
architecture how we wanted it,

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:48.920
we used Hyperparameter
Tuning in ML Engine,

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:52.020
another brilliant
feature of Google Cloud.

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:56.430
And so here, we were
able to tune and sort

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:59.890
of grid search our batch
size, learning rate,

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:03.340
number of layers, the shape
and size of our layers,

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:04.860
and our activation
function to find

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.990
the optimal, most
skillful set of those

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:09.675
to make our predictions.

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:18.840
So here is a slide that
we're really proud of.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:21.995
So as we said, we have 300
different product categories.

00:24:21.995 --> 00:24:24.960
The product categories are
really based on consumer needs,

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:27.210
so mops are a category.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:29.177
Dehumidifiers are
a category, where

00:24:29.177 --> 00:24:31.260
you've got a lot of products
that a retailer might

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:32.800
have within each category.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:37.200
But we're predicting demand for
mops, demand for dehumidifiers.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:41.460
And in the end, for our
post-storm predictions,

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:44.910
we were able to get
90% of our predictions

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:52.770
within $150 daily average error,
and that's based on hold-outs.

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.290
Meaning if we predict
it's going to be

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:58.800
$300 in sales, 68%
of the time, it's

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:04.680
going to be between
$250 and $350.

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:06.390
So pretty accurate there.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:07.380
We were really pleased.

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:09.450
Our client was really
pleased with the accuracy

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:12.540
on the hold-out set.

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:16.800
And I think that's a function
of a lot of time spent

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:19.110
in feature engineering,
data pipelining,

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:23.070
as well as the power of
deep neural networks.

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:29.900
And then ultimately, I think one
of the major challenges here--

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:31.805
and you'll hear about
it in all of the DevOps

00:25:31.805 --> 00:25:35.150
for ML conversations--
is actually getting this

00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:38.516
deployed and operationalized.

00:25:38.516 --> 00:25:40.140
Ultimately, this is
sort of a structure

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:45.030
we think is the best to keep
this going on a long term using

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:48.470
Container Registry, Kubernetes,
obviously source control.

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:50.180
BigQuery is where
all that data lives.

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:52.130
And then we stored
out all of our models

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:55.130
in Keras in h5 format
to cloud storage.

00:25:55.130 --> 00:25:58.400
We were able to call those
back and make those predictions

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:01.850
in, really, almost real time.

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:06.140
I mean each prediction
takes seconds to make,

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:08.510
and the interaction
from cloud storage

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:10.439
and BigQuery and your
compute environment

00:26:10.439 --> 00:26:11.480
is really seamless there.

00:26:18.810 --> 00:26:20.190
So I'm going to
call Dan back up.

00:26:20.190 --> 00:26:22.440
He's going to talk about how
we take these predictions

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:24.660
and actually make them
operationalized on the ground.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.010
DAN KOPP: Thank you, Matt.

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:34.720
OK, so we've got a very
accurate model now.

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:38.290
It's putting out numbers
that we believe in

00:26:38.290 --> 00:26:40.400
and that we're ready
to take action on.

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:42.250
So back to my point
earlier, great.

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:43.750
How do you fold this back in?

00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:44.800
What do you do with it?

00:26:48.257 --> 00:26:49.840
A lot of things have
to happen, still.

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:53.830
First of all, the person still
has to be in the loop here.

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:56.320
Like any model, this
is a prediction.

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:59.200
It has a confidence to it.

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:01.510
And the confidence
can be high or low.

00:27:01.510 --> 00:27:04.000
So somebody needs to
say, this one's wrong.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:06.050
We need to change it.

00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:08.530
There's also other factors
that come into play.

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:10.780
Maybe the prediction
is right on,

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:13.210
but the DC doesn't
have the inventory,

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:17.525
or there is an event coming
up at the store next month,

00:27:17.525 --> 00:27:19.900
and they don't want to sell
all the inventory this month.

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:22.120
A lot of other factors
that come into play.

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:23.770
The model doesn't
know everything.

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:26.069
A person has got to be involved.

00:27:26.069 --> 00:27:27.610
And then once the
person approves it,

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:29.650
it's got to get out the door.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:32.680
So it's things like, how do
you fit it into your ERP system

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:35.500
to cut a purchase order?

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:38.990
And how do you then
get it on a truck?

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:41.820
You might be shipping-- your
company might use pallets,

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.330
or you might use cartons.

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:46.920
How do you get the right number
into those units and on a truck

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:48.990
and out the door?

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:54.280
There's a lot of rounding that
goes on in these scenarios.

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:57.840
So I thought I would
walk through an example.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:01.240
Again, all of this
is done in BigQuery.

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:05.320
So say, like we mentioned
before, we're doing sales

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:07.880
at a category or grouping level.

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:09.850
In this case, I'll
use generators.

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:12.250
So let's say first
store, we know

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:15.430
that we're going to need
$10,000 in generators.

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:18.130
And we say that, because
we don't necessarily

00:28:18.130 --> 00:28:21.010
know if it's this particular
generator that people really

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:22.434
want to buy or that one.

00:28:22.434 --> 00:28:24.100
At the end of the
day, people will often

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:26.770
buy whatever generator is
there if their power is out.

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:29.680
And so we predict
at the group level.

00:28:29.680 --> 00:28:32.380
But we ship at the item level.

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:34.660
And so we've got to get
this down to an item.

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:36.160
And there's lots
of ways to do this.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:39.520
To turn this into
an individual SKU,

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:41.060
you can do a number of things.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:42.620
It depends on your organization.

00:28:42.620 --> 00:28:45.370
So maybe you want
to default to what

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:48.550
that store's customers prefer.

00:28:48.550 --> 00:28:52.240
Maybe they really love
the 5,500 watt generator.

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:55.840
Or maybe we're talking about
another category-- say,

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:57.250
shingles--

00:28:57.250 --> 00:28:59.960
where in this part
of the country,

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:02.680
they really like
architectural gray shingles.

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:05.110
But in another part of country,
they like tan shingles.

00:29:05.110 --> 00:29:08.050
And so you really have to
be able to fine tune which

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:11.925
actual products you're
going to send to the store.

00:29:11.925 --> 00:29:14.050
Obviously, you have to
check the store's inventory.

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:15.716
Maybe the store has
everything you need,

00:29:15.716 --> 00:29:18.250
and you don't need
to ship a thing.

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:21.160
So again, we're talking about
BigQuery taking this prediction

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:25.540
and going in real time,
checking store inventory.

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:29.230
Maybe they don't have enough,
but there's a truck on the way.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:33.130
You've got to know
that too, obviously.

00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:35.350
Maybe even after that,
you still need some,

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:38.560
and so you have to see
if your DC has enough.

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:40.900
Maybe your DC has
enough for your store,

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:45.340
but doesn't have enough for all
the stores that that DC serves.

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:48.790
What do you do in that case?

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:50.710
And then let's say
you figure that out.

00:29:50.710 --> 00:29:53.680
You've still get to
round for, how many

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:54.790
do you put on a pallet?

00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:57.280
How many pallets do
you put on a truck?

00:29:57.280 --> 00:29:59.740
Maybe the recommendation
is you need--

00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:01.780
what did we say here--

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:04.360
you need two pallets
of the 5,500 watt.

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:07.610
Well, maybe that was 1.9
pallets that you really needed.

00:30:07.610 --> 00:30:09.444
You're probably going
to round that to two.

00:30:09.444 --> 00:30:11.110
You're not going to
slow the truck down.

00:30:11.110 --> 00:30:14.110
You're not going to slow this
process down to get that right.

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:15.960
You're going to move and go.

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:18.250
So how do you handle
that logic very quickly?

00:30:18.250 --> 00:30:22.030
Supply chain organizations
do this stuff all the time,

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:27.010
and the more we can help
them get there faster,

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:29.920
the faster this
whole process works.

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:35.312
And so you see, at the end here,
the net SKU sales prediction

00:30:35.312 --> 00:30:37.270
ends up being significantly
different than what

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:40.520
came out of the model.

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:43.970
And so just to talk
about data volume here,

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:49.460
imagine for a large company,
the size of the table that

00:30:49.460 --> 00:30:54.840
has the SKU store inventory.

00:30:54.840 --> 00:30:58.050
Think of the same
thing for DC inventory.

00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:00.380
Think of the same thing for POs.

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:03.440
You've got to hit
billion row tables

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:06.674
over and over and over again,
and you've got to do it fast.

00:31:06.674 --> 00:31:08.840
And BigQuery was, again,
pretty awesome at doing it.

00:31:12.606 --> 00:31:15.540
Again, so I'm on the,
how do you operationalize

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:19.690
this, notion here.

00:31:19.690 --> 00:31:22.380
So you take a model
that's complex,

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:26.450
and we didn't build this
to run this forever.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:29.100
We built this to hand
over to our client.

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:33.120
And so they often
have a varying range

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:37.020
of skill sets and knowledge
about data science

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:41.160
to where you've got to tailor
your solution to the people who

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:43.230
are going to receive it.

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:45.930
Also, there's a
complexity issue here.

00:31:45.930 --> 00:31:49.750
We could probably get the
90% accuracy up to 95%

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:53.070
or higher, if we baked
every single thing

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:56.160
we could in the model, took
everything into consideration.

00:31:56.160 --> 00:31:59.181
But then we risk it being
so unwieldy and cumbersome

00:31:59.181 --> 00:32:01.680
that nobody would even remember
what all these configuration

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:03.420
parameters were.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:05.970
And so we had to right-size it.

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:07.940
And so these are
just some examples.

00:32:07.940 --> 00:32:10.279
And so these are some of
the tunable parameters.

00:32:10.279 --> 00:32:11.070
We call them knobs.

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:14.087
We gave them a lot of knobs
to turn of how they can say,

00:32:14.087 --> 00:32:14.670
you know what?

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:15.630
I want to adjust this.

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:16.546
I want to adjust that.

00:32:16.546 --> 00:32:18.810
Give them the right
level of granular control

00:32:18.810 --> 00:32:21.570
without having to crack open the
code and get their hands dirty.

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:23.945
They can do that if they want,
but if they don't, they've

00:32:23.945 --> 00:32:26.700
got a very easy way to
say, last shipment we sent,

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:29.400
it was a little too much, or
it was a little too little.

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:30.880
Let's make some changes.

00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:36.300
So you see some things like
max wind gust threshold.

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:40.322
Maybe there are some events
where we didn't really

00:32:40.322 --> 00:32:41.780
recommend anything,
because we said

00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:43.150
they didn't hit a threshold.

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:44.970
Well, we could maybe
lower that number,

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:47.142
and more events will pop up.

00:32:47.142 --> 00:32:48.600
And with that, I'm
going to pass it

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:51.150
back to my colleague,
Mr. Matt, who's

00:32:51.150 --> 00:32:52.359
going to show you some demos.

00:32:52.359 --> 00:32:54.483
MATT YATES: So ultimately,
what we're going to demo

00:32:54.483 --> 00:32:56.530
here is the end
user interface just

00:32:56.530 --> 00:32:59.274
so you can get an idea of how
this would be done in action.

00:32:59.274 --> 00:33:00.690
Most of these
numbers are made up,

00:33:00.690 --> 00:33:05.720
so they don't tie out completely
just for client confidentiality

00:33:05.720 --> 00:33:06.220
purposes.

00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:08.700
But this will give you an
idea of what we're looking at.

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:12.180
We decided to use
Tableau for these demos.

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:14.580
Love looking at-- working
with Tableau for just

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:16.730
a quick dashboarding tool.

00:33:16.730 --> 00:33:18.874
And so we're giving
a sense of both.

00:33:18.874 --> 00:33:20.790
How do you look at weather
data and understand

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:22.890
what's happening on
the ground at a store?

00:33:22.890 --> 00:33:26.190
How do you turn your weather
data into predictions?

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:28.530
And how should you
consume that as a user?

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:31.560
Ultimately, after you turn
those predictions into orders,

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:33.750
how do you track it?

00:33:33.750 --> 00:33:37.040
So what you're looking
at here is the weather

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:38.650
aggregated to a store level.

00:33:38.650 --> 00:33:42.330
This is actually for
Hurricane Irma, I believe.

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:45.250
So you'll see all the stores
listed there on the map.

00:33:45.250 --> 00:33:47.760
If you click on a store, you
can get a data table here

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:51.850
that shows over time,
some key weather metrics.

00:33:51.850 --> 00:33:56.730
So in this circumstance, wind
gust here was 119 miles an hour

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:58.335
on September 12 at noon.

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:04.660
For one of the Florida Keys,
that was the actual wind speed.

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:07.750
We've got a suite of metrics
here from total precipitation,

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:10.118
temperature, vegetation
rates, soil moisture

00:34:10.118 --> 00:34:11.909
that you could actually
go through and look

00:34:11.909 --> 00:34:14.190
at and understand
what was happening

00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:16.942
on the ground at each store.

00:34:16.942 --> 00:34:18.900
So I'll play this for
you, and you can actually

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:20.679
see the wind gust
go up the coast.

00:34:20.679 --> 00:34:23.760
And it's on loop here, so you'll
see it repeating over and over.

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:26.940
And that is all
wind gusts that we

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:29.400
would consider to
be an event that we

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:32.969
may need to consider potential
demand spikes because

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:35.049
of those events.

00:34:35.049 --> 00:34:37.090
So then once you know
that, you want to turn that

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:38.360
into your prediction.

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:42.730
So what you're looking at here
is the actual event prediction

00:34:42.730 --> 00:34:44.480
for an Irma example--

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:45.620
154 stores.

00:34:45.620 --> 00:34:48.340
I'm going to drill into
a single store here.

00:34:48.340 --> 00:34:51.429
And we'll look at,
for the products that

00:34:51.429 --> 00:34:55.420
are affected for this
store, the baseline sales

00:34:55.420 --> 00:34:59.350
that we would have expected
without an event, 146,000,

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:01.540
we think we're going to get
across all these product

00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:04.280
categories, $310,000
worth of sales.

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:06.430
We only have $135,000 on hand.

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:07.780
We've got $33,000 en route.

00:35:07.780 --> 00:35:11.320
Therefore, we need to order
$209,000 worth of product.

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:13.900
That's $209,000
aggregate product,

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:16.634
but it's split across
a ton of item classes.

00:35:16.634 --> 00:35:18.550
So let's drill into some
of these item classes

00:35:18.550 --> 00:35:20.380
and see what we need to order.

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:23.290
For ACs and fans, we need
to order $95,000 there.

00:35:23.290 --> 00:35:26.460
You can see we need 38 pallets,
and our mean prediction error

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:28.000
there is plus or
minus one pallet.

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:32.980
So if I'm a merchant, and I want
to be conservative about it,

00:35:32.980 --> 00:35:34.480
maybe I'll just
subtract a pallet.

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:35.480
Maybe I'll add a pallet.

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:36.700
Same thing for tarps here.

00:35:36.700 --> 00:35:39.880
Our prediction error is
really good, zero on tarps.

00:35:39.880 --> 00:35:43.210
On grills, not quite as
good, so we need 21 pallets,

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:45.250
and that's plus or minus two.

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:49.030
And then let's drill in here
on air quality dehumidifiers.

00:35:49.030 --> 00:35:51.760
So we need to send three of
those pallets, plus or minus

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:54.980
zero on average, so
pretty good prediction.

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:58.180
So the store typically
sells this 30 watt version

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:01.060
of that, 31% of the time.

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:03.910
But then in emergencies, you
typically send a 50 watt.

00:36:03.910 --> 00:36:05.830
So we let the client
choose, do you

00:36:05.830 --> 00:36:08.440
want to send 30 watt, which is
typically sold in that store,

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:10.139
or do you want to
send the 50 watt?

00:36:10.139 --> 00:36:12.430
So I'm not going to go into
the actual ordering process

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:15.540
here, just for
confidentiality purposes.

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:17.439
But then ultimately,
we turned those,

00:36:17.439 --> 00:36:19.480
how many do you need to
order, into an order form

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:23.120
that they could execute
on their system.

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:24.920
Then once you execute
that order form,

00:36:24.920 --> 00:36:26.874
you want to be able
to track that data

00:36:26.874 --> 00:36:28.790
and track those orders
all the way to a store.

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:33.031
So we built another dashboard
that utilized all of the orders

00:36:33.031 --> 00:36:35.030
after they were placed
all the way up until they

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:36.210
were delivered.

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:39.030
So some examples here
of, you can drill in,

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:41.030
just like you can on the
other Tableau dashboard

00:36:41.030 --> 00:36:43.640
we just saw, into a
particular product category

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:45.950
for a particular store,
and see exactly what's

00:36:45.950 --> 00:36:50.372
headed to that store in both
retail dollars and units.

00:36:50.372 --> 00:36:52.580
This could be used by the
merchandising organization,

00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:54.496
or it could be used by
the store to understand

00:36:54.496 --> 00:36:55.370
what's on its way.

00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:57.286
And with the delivery
ETAs and everything else

00:36:57.286 --> 00:37:01.570
right there on the page.

00:37:01.570 --> 00:37:03.280
So wrap this up.

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:05.530
Machine learning plus a
really deep understanding

00:37:05.530 --> 00:37:08.020
of integration with
business process

00:37:08.020 --> 00:37:10.720
made this a successful project.

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:13.930
Ultimately, I think
a lot of you guys,

00:37:13.930 --> 00:37:16.270
probably not in
the same category,

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:18.850
but there's something to be
considered here for the ability

00:37:18.850 --> 00:37:21.610
of how can you react
and appropriately

00:37:21.610 --> 00:37:26.200
respond to human emergencies,
whatever they may be,

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:29.840
and help people and change
people's lives for the better.

00:37:29.840 --> 00:37:32.115
So really appreciate your
time listening today.

00:37:32.115 --> 00:37:33.740
We'll hang out over
here in the corner,

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:35.440
if you want to
ask any questions.

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:38.490
And that concludes
our presentation.

